1
00:00:00,115 --> 00:00:03,218
I was one of those overachiever kids.

2
00:00:03,218 --> 00:00:04,636
12 things going at once,

3
00:00:04,636 --> 00:00:06,688
already dreaming of changing the world.

4
00:00:06,688 --> 00:00:11,176
And I was so excited to go to summer school at Yale

5
00:00:11,176 --> 00:00:12,628
when I was 16.

6
00:00:12,628 --> 00:00:14,046
I didn't say I was cool.

7
00:00:14,046 --> 00:00:15,464
(Laughter)

8
00:00:15,464 --> 00:00:18,650
The classes were tough and thought-provoking.

9
00:00:18,650 --> 00:00:21,920
But that's when something strange started happening to me.

10
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:24,022
I'd be sitting in class, completely engaged,

11
00:00:24,022 --> 00:00:27,859
when suddenly, a fog rolled in.

12
00:00:27,859 --> 00:00:30,245
My thoughts fuzzed, my senses dulled,

13
00:00:30,245 --> 00:00:32,297
the world blurred,

14
00:00:32,297 --> 00:00:36,084
like someone was slowly turning a dimmer switch on my brain.

15
00:00:36,084 --> 00:00:38,170
I'd pinch my thigh, tap my foot,

16
00:00:38,170 --> 00:00:41,506
anything to fight this overwhelming urge to sleep.

17
00:00:41,506 --> 00:00:44,860
Once I bit my tongue so hard it bled.

18
00:00:44,860 --> 00:00:47,496
And then the thought would hit me like a hammer.

19
00:00:47,496 --> 00:00:52,334
"If I don't go to sleep right now, I'm going to die."

20
00:00:52,334 --> 00:00:54,703
So somehow, quietly, I'd excuse myself,

21
00:00:54,703 --> 00:00:56,288
slip into a bathroom stall,

22
00:00:56,288 --> 00:01:00,142
lean my head against the wall and fall asleep.

23
00:01:00,142 --> 00:01:02,044
When I told my family doctor, she said,

24
00:01:02,044 --> 00:01:04,196
"You're busy. Busy people are tired."

25
00:01:04,196 --> 00:01:05,380
It felt dismissive,

26
00:01:05,380 --> 00:01:09,234
but also like some secret initiation into adulthood.

27
00:01:09,234 --> 00:01:11,803
I had big dreams, a lot to accomplish,

28
00:01:11,803 --> 00:01:15,490
so I figured I just had to get better at being awake.

29
00:01:15,490 --> 00:01:17,876
When I started working in professional politics,

30
00:01:17,876 --> 00:01:20,879
exhaustion was a badge of honor.

31
00:01:20,879 --> 00:01:23,348
The later you stayed up, the more coffee you drank,

32
00:01:23,348 --> 00:01:25,100
the more worthy you felt.

33
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:27,869
Society convinced us that if we were tired,

34
00:01:27,869 --> 00:01:30,839
we were doing it right.

35
00:01:30,839 --> 00:01:34,326
In 2008, I joined the Obama Advance team,

36
00:01:34,326 --> 00:01:36,144
the people responsible for everything

37
00:01:36,144 --> 00:01:39,064
from logistics to aesthetics on the campaign.

38
00:01:39,064 --> 00:01:40,749
But the thing about Advance,

39
00:01:40,749 --> 00:01:42,834
you're in a new city every four to six days,

40
00:01:42,834 --> 00:01:44,336
regularly pulling all-nighters,

41
00:01:44,336 --> 00:01:47,306
on behalf of the future president of the United States.

42
00:01:47,306 --> 00:01:49,591
The stakes were high, it was fast-paced,

43
00:01:49,591 --> 00:01:52,044
everyone was exhausted.

44
00:01:52,044 --> 00:01:58,000
I was surrounded by the best, the brightest and the busiest.

45
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,853
But I never saw them sneaking into the bathroom to nap like I did.

46
00:02:01,853 --> 00:02:03,422
(Laughter)

47
00:02:03,422 --> 00:02:05,490
I thought I must be lazy,

48
00:02:05,490 --> 00:02:10,012
that I lacked the willpower everyone else seemed to have.

49
00:02:10,012 --> 00:02:13,598
And underneath all that drive,

50
00:02:13,598 --> 00:02:18,920
I felt utterly alone in my inability to keep up.

51
00:02:18,920 --> 00:02:21,990
As the years passed, I kept bringing up my sleepiness to doctors

52
00:02:21,990 --> 00:02:26,144
who kept pushing the same glossy handouts on improving my sleep hygiene.

53
00:02:26,144 --> 00:02:29,131
My gut knew something else was going on.

54
00:02:29,131 --> 00:02:31,450
And then came the dreams.

55
00:02:31,450 --> 00:02:33,669
I'd wake up frozen in bed,

56
00:02:33,669 --> 00:02:35,904
convinced something terrifying was in the room.

57
00:02:35,904 --> 00:02:38,724
I could see it, but I couldn't move or scream.

58
00:02:38,724 --> 00:02:42,828
One doctor said it was just me manifesting stress from my job.

59
00:02:42,828 --> 00:02:45,914
One night I woke to a child whispering,

60
00:02:45,914 --> 00:02:49,668
"Can I hold your hand?"

61
00:02:49,668 --> 00:02:52,354
The next day I saged my entire bedroom,

62
00:02:52,354 --> 00:02:54,139
like, it never crossed my mind

63
00:02:54,139 --> 00:02:56,058
I might have a neurological disorder.

64
00:02:56,058 --> 00:02:58,860
(Laughter)

65
00:02:58,860 --> 00:03:01,196
At 35, I hit my wall.

66
00:03:01,196 --> 00:03:03,532
I could fall asleep, but I couldn't stay asleep.

67
00:03:03,532 --> 00:03:05,016
I was awake most of the night,

68
00:03:05,016 --> 00:03:07,519
falling back asleep just before my alarm went off.

69
00:03:07,519 --> 00:03:11,223
Most days I felt like a lunatic zombie, barely holding it together.

70
00:03:11,223 --> 00:03:12,657
I would drop things suddenly,

71
00:03:12,657 --> 00:03:15,143
in the middle of stressful meetings at work.

72
00:03:15,143 --> 00:03:19,014
At my wit's end, I begged my general practitioner for help.

73
00:03:19,014 --> 00:03:22,617
She ordered an at-home sleep test, which came back negative for sleep apnea.

74
00:03:22,617 --> 00:03:24,386
And I was told, "You're fine."

75
00:03:24,386 --> 00:03:25,904
I marched back into her office

76
00:03:25,904 --> 00:03:28,623
with a full defense that something else was wrong.

77
00:03:28,623 --> 00:03:30,625
She stopped me mid-sentence and admitted,

78
00:03:30,625 --> 00:03:33,795
"We're officially beyond both our knowledge of sleep."

79
00:03:33,795 --> 00:03:35,614
I was referred to a sleep specialist,

80
00:03:35,614 --> 00:03:40,035
which is a doctor trained to diagnose and treat sleep disorders.

81
00:03:40,035 --> 00:03:41,837
And two months later,

82
00:03:41,837 --> 00:03:45,056
after 19 years of symptoms,

83
00:03:45,056 --> 00:03:47,826
shame and self-doubt,

84
00:03:47,826 --> 00:03:49,644
I was diagnosed with narcolepsy,

85
00:03:49,644 --> 00:03:53,715
a chronic neurological condition of the brain's sleep-wake cycle.

86
00:03:53,715 --> 00:03:57,319
And before you say, "19 years, that's extreme."

87
00:03:57,319 --> 00:03:58,770
It's not.

88
00:03:58,770 --> 00:04:03,291
The average diagnosis period for narcolepsy is eight to 15 years.

89
00:04:03,291 --> 00:04:06,628
And that's because in medical schools worldwide,

90
00:04:06,628 --> 00:04:08,797
doctors receive, on average,

91
00:04:08,797 --> 00:04:14,136
less than three hours of education on sleep

92
00:04:14,136 --> 00:04:18,473
total across their entire medical training.

93
00:04:18,473 --> 00:04:21,560
No wonder no doctor thought my sleepiness was an issue

94
00:04:21,560 --> 00:04:24,846
or asked any follow-up questions.

95
00:04:24,846 --> 00:04:27,649
Narcolepsy has five distinct symptoms that, on their own,

96
00:04:27,649 --> 00:04:29,568
seem completely unrelated.

97
00:04:29,568 --> 00:04:33,155
Excessive sleepiness that can feel like you've been awake for 48 hours,

98
00:04:33,155 --> 00:04:35,357
even after a full night’s rest:

99
00:04:35,357 --> 00:04:39,044
nighttime hallucinations, sleep paralysis, disrupted sleep

100
00:04:39,044 --> 00:04:40,662
and sometimes cataplexy --

101
00:04:40,662 --> 00:04:43,648
a loss of muscle tone with strong emotions.

102
00:04:43,648 --> 00:04:45,834
Which is why I don't have slides in this talk,

103
00:04:45,834 --> 00:04:48,503
because I was afraid I would drop the remote.

104
00:04:48,503 --> 00:04:53,475
I never walked into a doctor’s office and listed them all together

105
00:04:53,475 --> 00:04:58,163
because there was no way for me to know they belonged to the same story.

106
00:04:58,163 --> 00:05:00,832
The same way there was no way for me to know

107
00:05:00,832 --> 00:05:03,935
my tired was different.

108
00:05:03,935 --> 00:05:07,489
But for one in five of us, our tired is different.

109
00:05:07,489 --> 00:05:09,007
While narcolepsy might be rare,

110
00:05:09,007 --> 00:05:12,844
it’s estimated [that] 20 percent of the population has a sleep disorder --

111
00:05:12,844 --> 00:05:15,163
everything from sleep apnea to chronic insomnia,

112
00:05:15,163 --> 00:05:16,598
restless leg syndrome

113
00:05:16,598 --> 00:05:19,684
and other conditions that quietly steal rest.

114
00:05:19,684 --> 00:05:22,971
And hundreds of millions of people worldwide

115
00:05:22,971 --> 00:05:25,290
are walking around exhausted,

116
00:05:25,290 --> 00:05:28,977
untreated and thinking it’s normal to feel this way.

117
00:05:28,977 --> 00:05:31,213
We've been taught to push through exhaustion

118
00:05:31,213 --> 00:05:33,381
instead of asking about it.

119
00:05:33,381 --> 00:05:36,001
This isn't an epidemic of laziness.

120
00:05:36,001 --> 00:05:39,421
It's a failure of diagnosis.

121
00:05:39,421 --> 00:05:42,173
We dismiss people with sleep disorders all the time.

122
00:05:42,173 --> 00:05:45,443
We think sleep apnea only affects middle-aged men with thick necks

123
00:05:45,443 --> 00:05:46,745
and square jaws.

124
00:05:46,745 --> 00:05:47,929
(Laughter)

125
00:05:47,929 --> 00:05:51,816
But it can affect anyone, at any age and any size.

126
00:05:51,816 --> 00:05:55,103
Now, thankfully, we've started to recognize that sleep is vital.

127
00:05:55,103 --> 00:05:59,074
One of the most important things you can do for your physical or mental health.

128
00:05:59,074 --> 00:06:03,245
When you get enough sleep, your body performs quiet miracles.

129
00:06:03,245 --> 00:06:07,382
Repairing cells, balancing hormones, clearing toxins from your brain.

130
00:06:07,382 --> 00:06:10,952
Sleep is where creativity is born, memories are filed

131
00:06:10,952 --> 00:06:14,322
and the subconscious gets to color outside the lines.

132
00:06:14,322 --> 00:06:17,375
Sleep is the engine of productivity.

133
00:06:17,375 --> 00:06:19,661
Now good sleep hygiene matters.

134
00:06:19,661 --> 00:06:22,597
But for the one in five of us living with a sleep disorder,

135
00:06:22,597 --> 00:06:25,350
sleep hygiene alone isn't a cure.

136
00:06:25,350 --> 00:06:27,702
No perfect bedtime routine, no weighted blanket,

137
00:06:27,702 --> 00:06:29,354
no lavender spray on your pillow

138
00:06:29,354 --> 00:06:32,157
will keep your airway open at night.

139
00:06:32,157 --> 00:06:36,962
Because when the problem is neurological, structural or biochemical,

140
00:06:36,962 --> 00:06:40,916
without medical intervention, your body pays the price.

141
00:06:40,916 --> 00:06:43,301
Aside from brain fog, loss of productivity

142
00:06:43,301 --> 00:06:45,754
and the dangers of drowsy driving,

143
00:06:45,754 --> 00:06:48,640
over time, when sleep disorders go undiagnosed,

144
00:06:48,640 --> 00:06:52,210
they silently break the body down from the inside out,

145
00:06:52,210 --> 00:06:54,546
fueling inflammation, accelerating aging,

146
00:06:54,546 --> 00:06:56,431
driving insulin resistance

147
00:06:56,431 --> 00:06:58,950
and raising the risk of heart attack,

148
00:06:58,950 --> 00:07:01,753
stroke [and] even Alzheimer’s.

149
00:07:01,753 --> 00:07:04,105
This isn't just exhaustion.

150
00:07:04,105 --> 00:07:07,876
It’s a slow erosion of health, focus

151
00:07:07,876 --> 00:07:10,195
and longevity.

152
00:07:10,195 --> 00:07:12,948
But it's also an identity issue.

153
00:07:12,948 --> 00:07:16,418
I didn't become a whole person until I was diagnosed with narcolepsy.

154
00:07:16,418 --> 00:07:18,320
Before that, I was working or sleeping.

155
00:07:18,320 --> 00:07:20,121
There was nothing in between.

156
00:07:20,121 --> 00:07:23,959
But with treatment, the world came alive to me.

157
00:07:23,959 --> 00:07:27,662
Colors got brighter, [and] I felt a full range of emotions.

158
00:07:27,662 --> 00:07:29,464
I started having hobbies.

159
00:07:29,464 --> 00:07:31,282
(Laughter)

160
00:07:31,282 --> 00:07:34,319
I found my voice.

161
00:07:34,319 --> 00:07:38,373
Before my narcolepsy diagnosis, I didn't think I was creative.

162
00:07:38,373 --> 00:07:41,910
In the last eight years of treatment, I've written two pilot scripts,

163
00:07:41,910 --> 00:07:44,195
[written] and published a whole book,

164
00:07:44,195 --> 00:07:45,797
became a keynote speaker

165
00:07:45,797 --> 00:07:49,701
and had articles published in national outlets.

166
00:07:49,701 --> 00:07:55,023
Before my diagnosis, my world felt extraordinarily limited.

167
00:07:55,023 --> 00:07:58,943
Now there's no limit to my capacity.

168
00:07:58,943 --> 00:08:02,247
So consider this your activation,

169
00:08:02,247 --> 00:08:05,300
your permission slip, your reclamation,

170
00:08:05,300 --> 00:08:09,738
your literal wake-up call to question not just your sleep,

171
00:08:09,738 --> 00:08:12,190
but how you feel during the day.

172
00:08:12,190 --> 00:08:14,759
If something is off, ask for help,

173
00:08:14,759 --> 00:08:16,161
demand answers,

174
00:08:16,161 --> 00:08:18,530
see a sleep specialist.

175
00:08:18,530 --> 00:08:20,498
Not just for your health,

176
00:08:20,498 --> 00:08:24,185
but for the idea, the project, the relationship,

177
00:08:24,185 --> 00:08:27,622
the version of you that's waiting to wake up.

178
00:08:27,622 --> 00:08:30,875
Because I want you to live your biggest,

179
00:08:30,875 --> 00:08:32,227
most beautiful life --

180
00:08:32,227 --> 00:08:33,595
full of color,

181
00:08:33,595 --> 00:08:35,597
curiosity and awe.

182
00:08:35,597 --> 00:08:39,584
Wild and wide awake.

183
00:08:39,584 --> 00:08:42,203
So I'm asking you,

184
00:08:42,203 --> 00:08:46,391
is your tired different?

185
00:08:46,391 --> 00:08:50,391
(Applause)
